Q3)  
     Problem:
The administrator needs to process 1 million log entries per day using only shell scripting. The main challenge is to process all entries within 2 minutes
while minimizing CPU,
memory, and disk usage.
Solution:
Use optimized Linux commands and avoid unnecessary loops.
Example:
   /bin/bash
LOGFILE="system.log"
grep "ERROR" "$LOGFILE" |
awk '{count[$5]++} END {
    for (key in count)
        print key, count[key]
}' > error_report.txt
Use:
grep "ERROR" system.log | sort | uniq -c > error_report.txt
Recommended Commands:
grep
Used to quickly filter required log entries.
awk
Used for fast text processing, field extraction, and counting.
sort
Used to organize matching records efficiently.
uniq
Used to count duplicate entries after sorting.
wc
Used to quickly count lines and entries.
Example:
wc -l system.log
grep -c "ERROR" system.log
Performance Optimization:
Avoid processing logs line-by-line using shell loops.
Use grep, awk, sort, and uniq because they are optimized compiled Linux utilities.
Process the log file in a single pipeline where possible.
Avoid unnecessary temporary files.
Use parallel processing only when required and when the system has multiple CPU cores.

Example:
time grep "ERROR" system.log | sort | uniq -c > error_report.txt
Justification:
grep provides fast pattern matching, awk performs efficient field processing, sort organizes data efficiently, and uniq removes duplicate records and produces
counts. Using pipelines reduces unnecessary disk I/O and avoids slow shell loops.
Therefore, an optimized pipeline using standard Linux commands can process 1 million log entries efficiently and is suitable for meeting the 2-minute requirement,
subject to the log size, hardware performance, and complexity of the processing.

Q4)
   Problem:
A faulty background process is consuming 90% of CPU resources. The system should not be restarted, and critical services must continue running normally.
Process Management Strategy
Identify the Faulty Process
Use top or ps to find the process consuming high CPU.
top
or
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head
Note the PID of the faulty process.
Monitor the Process
Use:
top -p PID
or
pidstat -p PID 1
This helps monitor CPU usage continuously.
Reduce Process Priority
If the process is not critical, reduce its priority using:
renice +10 -p PID
This allows critical services to get more CPU time.
Limit CPU Usage
Use cpulimit if available:
cpulimit -p PID -l 20
This limits the process to approximately 20% CPU usage.
Stop the Faulty Process
If the process continues causing problems, terminate it safely:

kill PID
If it does not stop:
kill -9 PID
Use kill -9 only as a last option.
Verify Critical Services
Check running services using:
systemctl --type=service --state=running
Monitor CPU usage again with:
top
Justification:
top and ps identify the high-CPU process. pidstat provides continuous monitoring. renice reduces its CPU priority without affecting other services.
cpulimit controls excessive CPU consumption. kill safely terminates the faulty process if required.
Thus, the faulty process can be identified, monitored, and controlled without restarting the system, while critical services continue operating normally.


Q5)
  Given:
Total physical RAM = 16 GB
Host OS requirement = 4 GB
Number of VMs = 5
Minimum RAM per VM = 2 GB
1. Calculate available RAM
RAM available for VMs:
16 GB − 4 GB = 12 GB
2. Allocate RAM to VMs
Minimum required for 5 VMs:
5 × 2 GB = 10 GB
So after giving every VM its minimum:
12 − 10 = 2 GB remaining
An efficient allocation is:
Resource	RAM
Host OS	4 GB
VM1	2 GB
VM2	2 GB
VM3	2 GB
VM4	3 GB
VM5	3 GB
Total	16 GB
3. Strategy:
Keep 4 GB permanently reserved for the host OS so the physical server remains stable.
Allocate 2 GB minimum to every VM.
Give the remaining 2 GB to VMs that run more demanding applications.
Use dynamic memory allocation/ballooning if supported by the hypervisor. This allows RAM to be moved between VMs according to workload.
Monitor RAM usage and avoid overcommitting memory heavily, because all 5 VMs must operate smoothly.
Conclusion:
The best basic allocation is 4 GB for the host + 2 GB each for three VMs + 3 GB each for two high-demand VMs = 16 GB. Dynamic memory can then adjust
the VM allocations when workloads change.
